<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edwin Kemmerer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Edwin Walter Kemmerer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edwin Kemmerer was an American economist at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeton-university">Princeton University</a> known as the 'money doctor'. In the 1920s he led financial missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and other countries, recommending central banks, the gold standard and budget reforms. His mission to Ecuador in 1926–1927 led to the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-bank-of-ecuador">Central Bank of Ecuador</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edwin-kemmerer.jpg" alt="Edwin Kemmerer" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Grantham Bain Collection (Library of Congress) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edwin_Walter_Kemmerer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Edwin Walter Kemmerer was born on 29 June 1875. He graduated with honours from Wesleyan University, where he received a Phi Beta Kappa key, and took his doctorate at Cornell University, where he taught from 1906 to 1912. At the age of 28 he was appointed financial adviser to the U.S. Philippine Commission. In 1912 he became a professor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeton-university">Princeton University</a> and the first director of its new International Finance Section.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Kemmerer helped design the U.S. Federal Reserve System in 1911, edited the American Economic Bulletin and the American Economic Review, and became president of the American Economic Association in 1926. Besides his missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> he advised the governments of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. The plans of such advisers aimed at an internationally connected system of stable currencies fixed in value to gold, and they were also written to win debtor countries the approval of much needed loans. Charles Dewey followed him in Poland, while Jeremiah Smith Jr and S. Parker Gilbert advised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and Germany.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> invited Kemmerer after the coup of May 1926 that brought General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Józef Piłsudski</a> to power, having first consulted Benjamin Strong, President of the Federal Reserve Bank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. Kemmerer stayed only a few months, while Dewey stayed for more than two years. His report described Polish agriculture, industry and communications in detail, but it proposed the same mechanism of automatic budget control that had been set up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>, with little regard to Polish conditions. Kemmerer admitted that a country following American advisers improved its chances of obtaining American capital on favourable terms.</p><p>He recommended a stabilisation loan of $15 million, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> negotiated $61 million with the American banks B.A. Tompkins and Bankers Trust. When lending from Wall Street dried up in mid-1928, the attempt to stabilise the Polish economy failed as the country sank further into political instability.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Kemmerer's books include Money and Credit Instruments in their Relation to General Prices (1907), Modern Currency Reforms (1916), High Prices and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deflation">Deflation</a> (1920) and Kemmerer on Money (1934). In 1927 he described his missions in the essay Economic Advisory Work for Governments, and his Gold and the Gold Standard appeared in 1944. He died on 16 December 1945.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edwin W. Kemmerer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Julian Revolution of 1925, a reformist government advised by the American economist <b>Edwin Kemmerer</b> created the Central Bank of Ecuador in 1927.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Scranton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Princeton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.41,-75.66&amp;year=1920" aria-label="Scranton on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Edwin Kemmerer"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5910 3973h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5910 3973h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.41,-75.66&amp;year=1920">What was here in 1920? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
